~ start
Chancellor Vogler: Our carmakers say yours are being fed by the state. Yours say the same of ours. The honest summary is that we are both right.
- Propose a joint industrial fund => joint_fund
- Offer a truce on state aid => truce
- Defend French industrial policy => defend
~ joint_fund
Chancellor Vogler: A fund we both pay into and neither controls. It has the elegance of a marriage of convenience.
- Put the fund into green steel => green_steel
- Put the fund into batteries => batteries
~ green_steel
Chancellor Vogler: Steel. The Ruhr will weep with joy and the Lorraine will ask why it was not consulted.
=> END
~ batteries
Chancellor Vogler: Batteries, then. The only thing Europe imports more of than gas is the argument about who should make them.
=> END
~ truce
Chancellor Vogler: A truce is cheap to announce and costly to keep. I will keep it for exactly as long as you do.
- Sign a written truce => truce_signed
- Keep it a gentleman's agreement => truce_verbal
~ truce_signed
Chancellor Vogler: In writing, then. Let the lawyers on both sides learn what it means to lose a hobby.
=> END
~ truce_verbal
Chancellor Vogler: A handshake, over a beer. The best treaties are those a ministry cannot find in its files.
=> END
~ defend
Chancellor Vogler: You defend it well. I would defend it too, if it were mine, and I would ask the Commission not to look.
- Say France will not apologise => no_apology
- Concede a little on the details => concede
~ no_apology
Chancellor Vogler: Then we will keep arguing, which is something we do well and, on balance, usefully.
=> END
~ concede
Chancellor Vogler: A small concession, quietly made. That is how this continent has always moved.
=> END
